  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 PM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nAt midday, the morning round of rain is exiting to the east while \nanother batch of scattered showers continues to develop and expand \nacross the western CWA while the remnants of an MCV slowly moves \nthrough central KS. That second round will progress eastward and out \nof the area by this evening. In the meantime, an H5 trough is digging\nover eastern MT into the High Plains of the Dakotas. That trough and\nan associated surface low will progress southeast into the heart of \nthe Midwest tomorrow. Thunderstorms still look to develop ahead of \nthe surface cold front this evening in southern SD and northern NE. \nMost CAMs show those storms entering north-central KS around midnight\nand advancing southeast across the CWA tonight. The overall threat \nfor severe weather still looks fairly limited due to increasing CIN \nand waning instability. However, a few strong winds gusts could \noccur, especially early in the event over north-central KS. \n\nShowers and storms exit to the east Friday morning as winds shift to \nthe northwest. The pressure gradient on the back side of a deepening\nsurface low over IA will make for a breezy afternoon, especially in \nnorth-central and far northern KS where winds could gust 30 to 35 \nmph. Temperatures will mainly be in the 80s but dew points will stay\nin the 70s across eastern KS. North winds remain breezy Saturday as \nthe surface low pivots east of the area, but higher pressure will \nbring in a cooler and drier air mass. Highs should top out in the low\n80s and dew points are expected to be about ten degrees cooler than \nFriday afternoon -- a noticeable improvement!\n\nLooking longer term, conditions stay dry through at least next \nTuesday with a lack of any storm systems moving across the central \nUS. High temperatures increase slightly each day beyond Saturday as \nthe surface ridge moderates and moves eastward. Highs will likely be \nback in the 90s by the middle of next week with humidity also \nincreasing into the end of next week.",
